# Exercise: Reading the Wall Clock
#
# `time.now` returns the current Unix timestamp — seconds since
# 1970-01-01 UTC.
#
# Stack effect: ( -- Int )
#
#   time.now    # → e.g. 1762636800
#
# Heads up: `time.now` is **seconds**, not milliseconds. There is
# no millisecond version of the wall clock — if you need
# sub-second precision for measuring durations, use `time.nanos`
# (next exercise but two).
#
# Your task: write `time-is-positive` — return true when
# `time.now` returns a positive integer. (It always will, on any
# machine whose clock is set after the Unix epoch, but the point
# is to wire `time.now` into a real expression.)
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: time-is-positive ( -- Bool )
    # Your code here
    false
;

: test-time ( -- )
    time-is-positive

    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
